  "strategy": "Approach and Rationale\nWe change the representation from grid points to a sparse transition representation. Instead of representing the function as a full grid, we use a set of transition points. Our new representation ensures pairwise distinct samples and optimizes over a low-dimensional manifold. We also implement a new objective function based on exact cross-correlation without FFT and enable multi-resolution optimization from coarse to fine. We maintain the integral constraint by adjusting the last transition point and diverging from the parent solution when necessary to explore new parameter spaces.\n\nMathematical Specification\nVariables:\n- `transitions`: A vector of sorted transition points in [0, 2] that ensures no duplicates\n- `mass`: The total integral of the function, initially set to 1.0\n- `target_mass`: The desired integral constraint (n_points/2.0)\n- `objective`: Minimize the maximum cross-correlation between h(x) and (1-h(x+k)) over all shifts k\n\nConstraints:\n1. Sorted distinct transitions with multiplicity enforcement\n2. Enforce integral exactly 1 by adjusting the last transition point if needed\n3. Implicit bounds: 0 \u2264 transition[i] < 2 and constant spacing initialization\n\nImplementation Procedure\n1. Initialize using provided parent or Thue-Morse sequence via upsampling\n2. For given n_points (128, 256, 512):\n   a. Sample `num_steps` points from a seed-specific sequence (Thue-Morse or Fibonacci)\n   b. Project onto the transition constraints using a projected gradient method\n   c. Continuously optimize on the transition manifold with projected steepest descent\n3. Compute the exact grid function from transitions\n4. Calculate the correlation through explicit summation over all lags\n5. Refine the solution using local optimization for each resolution level\n6. Fallback to parent solution if fitness doesn't improve\n\nSearch Choices and Variations\n- Use progressive resolution via `num_steps=[3, 4, 5, 6]` for coherence\n- Transition generation uses chaotic initialization with binary protection\n- Optimization tracks the best solution globally\n- Dynamically adjust step size based on the last improvement\n- Only switch resolutions if fitness improves significantly\n\nNumerical Robustness and Memory Use\n- Maintain a sorted list of transitions at all times\n- Project solutions to satisfy mass constraint by adjusting the last point\n- Use floating-point rounding to avoid duplicate points\n- Track elapsed time to enforce budget and dynamically scale steps\n\nRuntime Safeguards and Return Contract\n- Operate within 1100 seconds to ensure timely progress\n- Define candidate scoring using the exact correlation\n- Accept solutions that must be fully within [0,1] and integral 1\n- Rename existing functions and add new procedures for clarity",
